"""SE423 Chapter 3: notes that explain instead of repeating the slide. See common.apply_notes (None keeps a note)."""

NOTES = {
    6: [None,
        'It works because people want the reward, and it stops working if you run out of things to offer. **Reward** also requires that you actually control something they value, such as bonuses or interesting assignments.',
        None,
        'People follow you because you know the answer, even without a title. That’s why a senior developer can have more real **expert** influence over a team than their manager does.',
        None],
    8: ['New people are careful and polite, and they look to the leader for direction. **Forming** feels calm, but little gets done yet, because people are still working out what’s expected of them.',
        'Once people relax, they start to push back and test who has influence. **Storming** feels like something’s going wrong, but it’s a normal stage. The PM’s job here is to surface and manage the conflict, not suppress it.',
        'Arguments settle and the group agrees how it will work, such as how decisions get made. **Norming** is when people start saying “we” rather than “I” and accept each other’s strengths.',
        'People stop spending energy on how to work together and spend it on the work. **Performing** is what you’re aiming for, and the team needs little supervision at this point.',
        'People may feel loss when a good team breaks up, so a proper ending matters: **adjourning** is when you wrap up and capture what was learned. Order: form → storm → norm → perform → adjourn.'],
    9: ['A production outage isn’t the time for meetings and approvals. **Problem resolution** needs a small group that can decide fast. Once it’s fixed, the team usually disbands, which is why it’s a **temporary task force**.',
        'You can’t order someone to have a new idea. **Creativity** needs freedom to try and fail, so a **looser hierarchy** and mixed backgrounds help, and punishing mistakes would kill it.',
        'Here the plan already exists, so success depends on precise execution. **Tactical execution** favors clear roles and **tight controls**, and creative freedom would only get in the way. (The instructor noted she spent time explaining this slide.)'],
    11: ['Everything else in the pyramid depends on this. If people don’t feel safe, they hide problems and avoid arguments, so **trust** comes first as the foundation.',
        'Most people assume that trust means “you’re reliable”. This kind goes further: it means being able to say “I messed up” and know nobody will use it against you. This list was a real midterm question, so learn **vulnerability-based trust**.',
        'Words can’t build it fast, as people need to see you keep your word several times. So **it takes time**, and since one broken promise can undo months, it must be **maintained**.',
        'Knowing how a teammate tends to work removes a lot of misunderstanding: “she’s direct, not rude”. **Behavioral profiling** is useful as a conversation starter, not as a label, and it lets the PM give people work that suits them.'],
    12: ['A title makes people obey, but it doesn’t make them trust you. You have to show them through what you do, so trust is **earned, not granted**, starting from a natural wariness.',
        'If people can’t tell you things, you find out about problems only when it’s too late. So **open communication** is risk management, and not just being friendly.',
        'If the PM hides their own mistakes, everyone learns to hide theirs. **Be fallible** by owning an error openly, because that makes it safe for others to do the same.',
        'People watch what you do far more than what you say. A PM who asks for punctuality and arrives late has told them what’s really expected. That’s why you **lead by example**.',
        'Nobody expects perfection, but they do expect honesty. Telling people in advance that you’ll miss a **commitment** keeps trust, while a surprise and an excuse damages it.',
        None],
    14: ['Think of healthy debate as a fight about ideas where nobody’s attacking the person. It only works if you trust that disagreement won’t be used against you, which is why **healthy conflict** sits above trust.',
        'If everyone is always comfortable, tough topics probably aren’t being raised. So some **uncomfortable** moments are a good sign, not something to fix.',
        'What’s normal in one team feels rude in another. Agreeing the rules up front, such as “challenge the idea, not the person”, stops arguments about how to argue. That’s what **conflict norms** are for.',
        'If nobody challenges a weak design because it would feel awkward, the mistake ships. That’s the cost of **avoidance**, and **groupthink** is what it looks like: everyone nods, and nobody really agrees.'],
    15: ['This asks “what’s really causing this?” and tackles that. It takes longer, but the problem doesn’t come back, which is why **confronting** isn’t the same as being aggressive.',
        None,
        'These two feel peaceful, but the cause stays there and tends to return bigger. **Withdrawal** can buy time when emotions are high. **Smoothing** can suit minor issues, or when the relationship matters more than the point.',
        'Each side’s view contributes something, so you may end up with a better answer than either started with. **Collaborating** takes time, so save it for decisions that matter.',
        'Sometimes there’s no time to discuss, like during an outage. **Forcing** gets a quick decision, but people who were overruled won’t feel committed, so use it rarely.',
        'Notice the first step: many teams solve the wrong problem because they jump to answers. The final step is the one often skipped: **review** whether it actually fixed the issue.'],
    16: ['Conflict that’s ignored doesn’t go away, as it comes back in other forms, such as people refusing to cooperate. The difference is whether a team **resolves** it.',
        'People can disagree and still work things out. It’s **conflict** once they’ve dug in, and each stops listening. Spotting that shift tells you when to step in.',
        'Taking sides only spreads the fight and splits the team, so others stay **neutral**. The **conciliator** helps both sides listen again until it’s an ordinary disagreement once more.',
        None],
    17: ['Ever left a meeting and found that everyone understood something different? **Commitment** fails that way, so a decision needs to be both understood and accepted.',
        'A simple habit fixes most of this: end each discussion by stating what’s been decided and who does what next. Without that **clarity**, people act on their own assumptions.',
        'People can accept a decision they argued against, as long as they feel it was fair and their view was heard. That’s why **buy-in** comes after debate rather than instead of it.',
        'Waiting for everyone to agree can freeze a team. Great teams argue, decide, and then back the decision (“disagree and commit”). A common trap: “commitment requires everyone to agree” is false, because **buy-in does not require consensus**.'],
    19: ['If only the manager enforces standards, you have one person chasing everyone. When teammates say “this isn’t up to what we agreed” to one another, problems get fixed faster. That’s accountability **among peers**.',
        'If the PM lets poor work slide, the team learns that standards are optional. So the leader needs a **willingness to confront** it, and doing that respectfully shows others how.',
        'Reviews work because the facts are in front of everyone. Note the tone in the slide’s example, about the commitment and what happened rather than who failed, in **meetings and regular reviews**.'],
    20: [None,
         'It’s worth a mention because some companies use it, so you won’t be confused if you see **RASCI**. It’s for people who help out on a task without being responsible for it.',
         None],
    21: ['If nobody clearly owns a task, it tends to fall through the cracks, and two people may each assume the other has it. A **RAM** makes that visible, and **RACI** is just one way of filling one in.',
         'During **planning** you assign the work, and during **tracking** you can see where a task has no owner. So it keeps its value after the first week.',
         'On a large project you may not assign to named people yet, so you can start with a **team or department**. Row totals show if one person or task is overloaded.'],
    23: ['A RAM says who is doing what, while a **skills matrix** says who is able to. Use it before assigning work, so you aren’t guessing about abilities.',
         None],
    24: ['Everything below this exists for one reason. A team can have trust and good meetings and still have failed if it doesn’t deliver, so it’s judged by **results**.',
         'A developer might want a clever feature for their CV, and a department might want its own goal. **Team results** come first, or everyone wins individually and the project loses.',
         'If a goal lives in a document nobody opens, it can’t guide daily decisions. A public, **visible** goal also makes it awkward for anyone to quietly drift from it.'],
    26: ['These are the two extremes. A **democratic** team gets better buy-in but decides slowly, while an **autocratic** one decides fast but depends on one person’s judgment.',
         None],
    27: ['Why is it so common? It’s simple, so everyone knows who to ask and who answers to outsiders. If a question doesn’t mention anything special, the **business team** is the safe assumption.',
         'One lead keeps responsibility clear, while the rest of the team stay equal. **Strong** suits a new or inexperienced team, and **loose** suits a skilled one. It’s this flexibility that makes it general.',
         'Trust has to come first. A new team asked to decide everything together would stall, so moving to a **democratic team** is something a team grows into.'],
    28: ['Nobody hands down a decision, so people commit to it, and it takes longer. That’s the trade-off behind reaching **consensus**: strong buy-in in exchange for speed.',
         'Someone still has to talk to the outside world, but that person isn’t the boss. As an **external interface**, they pass on information, and the team decides who does what.',
         'People take the work they’re best at, rather than getting tasks by rank. That only works when everyone knows one another’s **ability and experience**, as you can’t allocate fairly otherwise.',
         'If some members are beginners, they can’t judge decisions well and will either stay quiet or slow things down. So this is the condition to remember: **all members experienced and competent**.'],
    29: ['Agile teams estimate and assign their own work, because they’re closest to it. That’s why they count as **democratic** variants, though someone like a product owner still sets priorities.',
         None],
    30: ['Why would you build a team this way? Some people are far more productive, so the idea is to free your best person from everything except the hardest code, like the **surgeon** in an operating room.',
         'If the chief is ill or quits, everything depends on them. The **backup** exists to remove that risk, and also gives the chief someone able to catch their mistakes.',
         'Each role removes one kind of distraction from the chief: paperwork, admin, tools, language questions. You don’t need to memorize details beyond the fact that they’re **supporting roles**, though the list is fair for a listing question.',
         'Real superstars are rare, and those around them may resent the imbalance. So despite the appeal, it’s **difficult to achieve**. It can suit creative projects or tactical execution.'],
    31: ['Think of a core group that stays, with others joining for particular parts. That **kernel** keeps the design consistent, as a few minds decide the system’s shape.',
         None,
         'If someone can do the work of several people, the efficient choice is to remove obstacles from them. That’s the logic of a **supporting environment**: everyone else exists to help them produce.'],
    32: ['The whole model assumes you have a superstar. An average lead in this role becomes a bottleneck, so if exceptional people are **hard to find**, it fails.',
         'If one person gets the praise for everyone’s work, the rest may stop caring or even work against them. That’s why **resent** is on this list: the human side can sink the team.',
         'So much depends on two people that losing both, even temporarily, stops the project. This is **key-person risk**: the same thing a skills matrix would warn you about.',
         None],
    33: ['Distance from the main organization removes interruptions and rules, and the team feels the result is really theirs. That’s the appeal of **skunk works**, and the reason for **high ownership**.',
         'If they’re off on their own, nobody can easily tell how it’s going. Fine for exploring a new idea, but a narrow, well-defined job doesn’t need this freedom, and **little visibility** would just be a risk.',
         'Compare the two: skunk works is about freedom to explore, while **SWAT** is about precise skills for a known goal. They already work well together, so they can be dropped into a problem and start immediately.'],
    34: [None,
         'With that many paths, informal chat can’t keep everybody informed. A **hierarchy** reduces the paths, since people talk within small groups and leads talk to each other.',
         'Why so small? In a small team, everyone knows what’s going on and feels their work matters, and that’s what builds **cohesiveness**. The other roles sit around this core so it stays small.'],
    36: ['Equipment can be replaced, but people carry the knowledge and make the decisions. Calling them the **most important assets** means a PM should plan for people as carefully as for tasks.',
         None],
    37: ['Notice that “using available people” is a real limit: you rarely get to choose your ideal team. And **motivating** comes up here because a plan only happens if people want to do it.',
         'Plans and estimates can look purely technical, but “how fast” depends on who’s doing it. The same task can take one developer a day and another a week, so **estimating** is about people too.',
         '**Controlling** doesn’t mean micromanaging; it means knowing whether the work is on track. **Organizing** is about who works with whom and how, which shapes how well they do.'],
    38: ['**Needs-based** means that to motivate someone, you first ask what they’re lacking. The same reward can motivate one person and do nothing for another whose need is different.',
         'Think of the groups as moving outward: staying alive, feeling valued, then belonging. A manager has most influence on **personal** and **social** needs, since those arise at work.',
         None],
    39: ['A paying job with a safe office usually covers the basics. So a manager can start higher up, which is why **physiological and safety needs** aren’t the usual focus.',
         'Once the basics are covered, what makes people try harder is belonging, feeling respected and growing. These are the needs a manager can really act on, so **social, esteem and self-realization** matter most.',
         None],
    40: ['People work better with colleagues they know. Break rooms and casual chats look like wasted time, but they’re how **social** needs get met and how information spreads.',
         'People want to feel their effort was noticed. A specific “thank you” in front of the team can matter as much as a bonus, so **recognition** doesn’t have to be expensive.',
         'Here people want to grow and be trusted. So **training** and real **responsibility** motivate strong staff, while doing the same simple task for years drives them away.'],
    41: ['Maslow suggests everybody wants the same things in the same order, and real people aren’t like that. Two teammates at the same level can want very different things, hence **personality type**.',
         None,
         'The same project might be a means to a promotion or to buying a house. **Self-oriented** isn’t a criticism, but it tells you these people respond to personal benefit.',
         'For them, an isolated task with no contact is demotivating, even if it’s interesting. **Interaction-oriented** people keep a team talking, and that has real value.'],
    42: [None,
         'Even someone working mainly for money may stay because they like the team. So building a good **group and culture** is a motivation tool that doesn’t cost much.'],
    43: ['Real software is too big for one person, however talented. Working out how people fit together is therefore central, because this is a **group activity** by necessity.',
         None,
         'You rarely get to hand-pick a dream team, so plan around who you actually have. That’s why **group composition** is listed as limited: adapt the work to people’s strengths.'],
    44: [None,
         'Don’t plan a person’s time as if they code all day. With only **30%** alone, an estimate that assumes eight hours of solo work will be far too optimistic.'],
    45: ['Mixing types is deliberate. People with identical motivations tend to clash or leave gaps, just like a team of only goalkeepers, which is why having the **same motivation** is a problem.',
         'Each extreme has a weakness on its own: nobody coordinates, everybody competes, or nobody finishes. That’s why you look at the mix, not only at individuals: **task**, **self**, **interaction**.',
         'Since most engineers enjoy the work itself, you’ll often lack people who keep communication going. So value the **interaction-oriented** member, even if they write less code.'],
    46: ['A manager with a title can be ignored in practice if nobody respects them, while a respected senior developer gets followed. So **respect** is what actually makes a leader.',
         'Good engineers aren’t always good at budgets and reports, and vice versa. Splitting the role into **technical** and **administrative** leaders lets each person do what they’re good at.',
         'Skilled people often know more than their manager about the problem. Letting them contribute to decisions gets better answers and more commitment. That’s why **democratic leadership** works in software.',
         None],
    47: ['When people care about the group, they help each other and protect its standards, rather than only their own task. That’s what **cohesive** means in practice.',
         'Standards the group sets for itself get followed, while rules imposed from outside are often dodged. Working closely also means people quickly learn what they don’t know, rather than hiding it, which is how **ignorance** shrinks.',
         'If only one person understands a module, you have a risk. When people **learn from each other**, the team can cope with someone being away.',
         None],
    48: ['You can’t simply order people to bond. A blaming **organizational culture** works against it, and some personalities need more time, so treat it as something to encourage.',
         'A shared name or space sounds trivial, but it gives people something to belong to. **Group identity** is what turns individuals into “us”.',
         None],
    49: ['A close team defends its members, which helps morale. But **loyalty** can also stop people from speaking up when the group is wrong.',
         None,
         'A team can’t easily see its own blind spots, so bring in someone who isn’t invested. That’s what **external involvement** does: outsiders can ask the awkward questions.'],
    50: ['If one developer changes a design and nobody hears about it, others build on something that no longer exists. So **status** and **design decisions** must be shared, and sharing them also makes people feel included.',
         'Juniors may have the answer but stay quiet. A good PM deliberately asks them first, so the **status** of a senior person doesn’t shut the discussion down.',
         'If everyone is quiet, nothing gets raised, and if everyone is dominant, nobody listens. A mix of **personalities** helps conversations work, the same point as slide 45.',
         'If everything must pass through one person, they become a traffic jam, and messages get distorted along the way. Direct **channels** are usually better.'],
    51: ['More people mean many more communication paths, so past a certain size you spend more time coordinating than working. That’s why you **break big projects down** instead.',
         'With so few people, formal roles and procedures would only slow things down. An **informal, democratic** setup works because people can simply talk to each other…',
         '…while a **chief-programmer** team takes the opposite view: if skills are scarce, arrange the team so your strongest people do the critical work.'],
    52: ['Hiring mistakes are expensive and hard to undo, as a poor fit slows down everybody. Good selection is a **major responsibility** that prevents problems no process can fix later.',
         'Each source has a weakness: a **CV** is written to impress, and an **interview** is a short performance. That’s why you use several, and why meeting co-workers matters: they’re the ones who’ll work with this person.',
         'Don’t assume a test score predicts good work, since experts disagree. If an option says these tests are proven to work, it’s wrong: the slide says there’s **no agreement**.'],
    53: ['A developer who doesn’t understand banking can write clean code that does the wrong thing. That’s why **application domain** knowledge often matters more than knowing a specific language.',
         'Good programmers pick up a new language fairly quickly, so it’s only critical when there’s no time to learn. **Platform** knowledge counts when you’re working close to the hardware. A common trap: it is not the most critical factor.',
         'A degree tells you what someone has probably been taught, and how well they can learn. As real work builds up, it says less, which is why **education** counts for less over time.',
         'Software is a people job, and half of it is interaction (slide 44). Technology also changes constantly, so **communication** and **adaptability** can outlast any one technical skill.',
         'You can’t measure these from a CV, so they’re hard to judge, but a bad fit can harm a whole team. There’s no ideal “programmer **personality**”; what matters is whether they fit this team.'],
    54: ['People who are too hot, or who can’t see properly, work worse and get tired faster. So the **workplace** is a real productivity lever, and cheap compared with salaries.',
         'These aren’t luxuries, since people concentrate better when they’re comfortable and undisturbed. **Privacy** is singled out because of how much focused work depends on it.',
         'This one isn’t optional, because it’s about people’s wellbeing and is often a legal duty. **Health and safety** covers the basic conditions a workplace must meet.'],
    55: [None,
         'Windowless rooms affect mood over time. It seems a small thing, yet **natural light** comes up often in research on satisfaction at work, and it costs little to consider.',
         'Allowing people to arrange their own space and way of working tells them they’re trusted. **Personalization** helps them feel at home and work the way that suits them.'],
    56: ['Each interruption costs more than the interruption itself, since it takes time to get back into a hard problem. That’s why **individual offices** raise productivity, even with their cost.',
         None],
    57: ['Words shape how we think. Calling people “resources” puts them in the same category as desks and servers, which comes from the **economic** origin of the term.',
         'If you see people as parts, you assume one programmer can simply replace another, and that’s rarely true. Treating them as **interchangeable** also makes them feel disposable, so the good ones leave.',
         'Treating people as partners changes how you act: you ask instead of telling, and develop people instead of using them up. **Psychological safety** then lets them speak up, which protects the project.'],
    58: ['Think of these as different jobs: **interpersonal skills** are how you deal with one person, and **shaping culture** is how you influence how the whole team behaves…',
         '…**managing** is about getting today’s work done, while **making people better** is about what they’ll be able to do next year…',
         '…and **leadership** ties these together, by giving the team a direction they want to follow.'],
    59: ['Too little and people work on outdated information; too much and they stop reading. **Effective communication** means a known way of sharing, so everyone gets what they need, on time.',
         'A PM often needs help from people who don’t report to them. They can’t order it, so **influencing the organization** depends on credibility built earlier.',
         'Exam trap: someone with no title can lead and a manager can fail to. **Leadership** is about whether people choose to follow you toward a goal, which has **little to do with authority**.'],
    60: ['Money works up to a point, but it fades quickly and can’t make a job interesting. The surprising point is that **financial incentives** are among the weakest motivators, so try recognition, growth and meaningful work first.',
         'Differences may only be perceived, so listening can dissolve them. A PM rarely has the power to simply impose an outcome, so **negotiation** is an everyday tool, not a rare event.',
         'Most project problems are poorly defined at first. So the first skill is working out what’s really wrong, and then **progressive elaboration** means solving complex problems in steps rather than all at once.'],
    61: ['You can’t build a culture that fights the wider company for long. So learn how your **organizational culture** treats projects first, and shape your team’s habits within those limits.',
         'Some companies have impressive process documents that nobody follows. Knowing whether **real** practice matches the “fanciful” version tells you which rules you actually need to work with.',
         'Someone’s education and past experience shape how they work and what they’ll accept. A person’s **background** can tell you what kind of role they’d do well in, so you need to know your people.',
         'People tend to play a natural part in a team, whatever their job title. Knowing each person’s **cultural role** lets you give them work that suits it, which the next slide explains.'],
    62: ['These describe how people behave in a team, not their job title. A tester may be the team’s **expert** or its **charger**, and a healthy team needs a mix…',
         None,
         'Don’t confuse these with the cultural roles: these are assigned jobs. The **team leader** looks after people, and the **development leader** looks after the product, and on a small team one person may do both.',
         'It’s easy to label a critic as a problem, but they may be the only one seeing a risk. Being **non-judgmental** means asking what each role can contribute, rather than trying to change who they are.'],
    63: ['A young PM may struggle where age earns respect, regardless of the org chart. So **authority** doesn’t only come from a title, and your authority in one culture may not carry over to another.',
         'What’s normal in one country may be illegal in another, and a change of government can change the rules. So on international projects, **local laws and customs** are real planning inputs.',
         'If half the team sleeps while the other half works, every question can take a full day to answer. These **mundane** details can quietly cause much of the delay on a global project.'],
    64: ['Culture problems can kill a project just as a technical flaw can, and yet nobody tracks them. Treat team culture as you would **technical issues**: look for it, discuss it and fix it.',
         'People who are unsure of their role either overlap or leave gaps. **Clear roles** stop that, and telling someone you value their style helps them accept the role.',
         'People work better when they know the rules for hard moments. The slide’s example shows it: equal in normal times, and one decider in a crisis. Stating your **view of the team** up front avoids a fight later.',
         'It’s far easier to deal with one person dominating early than after others have stopped talking. So **recognize** the warning signs, and act before they harm the team.'],
    65: ['A role that seems fine on day one may turn out badly, and people rarely volunteer that they’re unhappy. Keep asking about **satisfaction**, especially **during launch**, when a change costs almost nothing.',
         'Don’t assume a job title means someone can do the task. A **role mismatch** shows up late as missed dates, and checking early is much cheaper than finding out after the schedule has slipped.',
         'You won’t find team mood in a report. Watch how people talk in meetings. Your behavior sets the tone too, so the **cultural climate** responds to your own example.'],
    66: ['A project rarely fails because the technology was impossible. It’s more often poor communication or a team that doesn’t work together, and that’s what **sociological** means here.',
         None,
         'Promoted engineers tend to treat people as a puzzle with a correct answer. But people aren’t logical systems, so one person’s **idiosyncrasies**, like needing quiet, can decide whether they do great work.'],
    67: [None,
         'Criticizing someone’s ability makes them defensive, and they stop listening. Discussing the **process** puts everyone on the same side against the problem, as the slide’s two sentences show.',
         'Tricking or pressuring people may work once, but it destroys trust. **Coordinate** by explaining why, and if someone’s effort is going to the wrong place, redirect it rather than discouraging them altogether.',
         'People notice when you’re protecting your position rather than helping. When your attention is on what the project and team need, they respect you without being told to. That’s why you **focus on needs**.'],
    68: ['Factory methods assume that identical work gets repeated. Software is different each time, so applying **production-line** thinking to it causes the errors listed below.',
         'On a line, an error is a defect, while in design it’s often how you learn. Someone staring out a window may be solving your hardest problem, so treating that as **goofing off** drives out your best thinking.',
         'A new person takes months to reach full speed, so people aren’t **interchangeable** like machine parts. Plans that assume a smooth steady state ignore the expensive start and end of work.',
         'Strict rules suit repeated tasks, but software keeps meeting new problems. If trying something new isn’t allowed, you won’t find better ways, so **eliminating experimentation** is harmful.'],
    69: ['It’s usually the first thing dropped under pressure, unless it’s a stated goal. Treating **professional development** like any other goal means time and money get set aside for it.',
         'You need both. **Short-term** learning helps this project now, while **long-term** learning keeps people growing, and it’s a large part of why good staff stay.',
         'People work harder at goals they chose. A **competency framework** just gives them a useful list to pick from, so that “improve” turns into something specific.',
         'Most people don’t know where their time really goes, and the results often surprise them. It only helps if it’s safe, so stress that this is for their own **awareness**, not for monitoring.'],
    70: ['A weekly timesheet is too rough to teach you anything. Tracking **daily** gives a developer real evidence, such as how long testing actually takes compared with what they guessed.',
         'With your own numbers, your next estimate gets better, and you stop promising what you can’t deliver. That’s how the **PSP** helps developers **make commitments they can meet**.'],
    71: ['Having a title doesn’t get a group to solve hard problems. **Mobilize** is the key word: people have to want to take it on, so it’s wrong to equate leadership with authority.',
         'If the problem has a known fix, the team just needs someone who knows it. **Technical leadership** is direct and efficient, and asking “what do you all think?” would just waste time.',
         'If nobody knows the answer, a leader giving orders is only guessing. **Adaptive leadership** helps the group learn its way there, and good questions do more than instructions.',
         'When people are exploring, it’s easy to drift. A **clarity of purpose** gives everyone a reason to keep going without a clear path, and lets the leader pull a discussion back.'],
    72: ['Ask first: does anyone know how to solve this? The answer decides which **approach** fits, and using the wrong one either wastes time or gives false confidence.',
         'Technical says “here’s what I see for us”, and it works because they know the way. Adaptive doesn’t have that map, so it uses a **sense of purpose**: why it’s worth working through.',
         None],
    73: ['Neither style is always better, so diagnose first. Fixing a known bug doesn’t need a discussion, and a brand-new product can’t be directed by a checklist. **Known means** is the deciding question.',
         'One project contains both: routine deployment work and a new algorithm nobody’s tried. So a PM has to switch between them, which is why a **mix** matters.',
         'A leader doesn’t need to handle everything personally. **Delegation** lets the person who knows the answer lead that part, which frees the leader to work on the problems nobody has solved.'],
    74: ['Exam trap: consensus doesn’t require full agreement. People can accept a decision they’d have made differently if they feel they had a fair hearing. That’s why it’s about being **heard**.',
         'On a five-person team, a 3–2 vote leaves two people who may quietly stop trying, and you need all five. That’s why **majority rule** can do harm, even though it seems fair.',
         'Everyone being heard doesn’t mean every argument is good. The leader should strengthen the good points and gently remove the bad ones, because they must **curate** what the decision rests on.'],
    75: ['Don’t start with solutions: agree on the problem first, and collect options before anybody defends one. The order is what keeps the **discussion** from turning into a contest.',
         'People who were simply overruled will resist later. Spending time to **persuade** now costs less than dealing with resistance during delivery, which is why the extra time pays off.',
         None],
    76: ['The same habits that build trust are how you lead day to day. If a question mixes these slides, the answer is the same, as this is a **reprise**.',
         'A nervous leader makes a team nervous. **Be confident**, without pretending you can do it alone, and you’ll get more from people who feel their talents are being used.',
         'A good relationship helps, but a friend may avoid saying hard things. You must sometimes give tough feedback, and that needs **boundaries**, even with people you like.'],
    77: ['Asking for what people can’t realistically do leads to burnout and resignations. Knowing the **human factors** tells you what’s reasonable to demand.',
         'A good exam summary of slide 53. **Domain experience** and adaptability often count for more than knowing a specific language.',
         'The reason is communication paths: small groups share information easily. **Status** matters because juniors may stay quiet, even when they’re right.',
         'Don’t wait for a culture problem to show up as a missed deadline. If you **monitor** early, you can act while it’s still cheap to fix. Your environment also affects output every day.'],
}
